var e=`<p>A postgraduate diploma takes less time and costs less than a master&#39;s degree, which makes it attractive to anyone in a hurry. Whether it is the right choice depends almost entirely on what you need it for.</p>
<h2>The structural difference</h2>
<p>A master&#39;s degree is normally two years, carries the full postgraduate credit weight, and is a degree. A PG diploma is typically one year, carries fewer credits, and is a diploma.</p>
<p>That last distinction sounds pedantic and is the whole issue. Wherever a requirement is written as &quot;a postgraduate degree&quot;, a diploma does not satisfy it, however good the diploma is.</p>
<h2>When a diploma is the right answer</h2>
<p>When you want a specific competence rather than a credential: a defined skill set in a year, to do your current job better or to move sideways into a related function.</p>
<p>When you already hold a master&#39;s degree and want to add a specialisation without repeating a full programme.</p>
<p>When the requirement you are satisfying is your own, rather than an HR criterion written in a rule.</p>
<h2>When only a master&#39;s degree will do</h2>
<p>When a promotion criterion, recruitment rule or notification specifies a postgraduate degree. Read the wording literally; this is the most common expensive mistake in this comparison.</p>
<p>When you may pursue a doctorate later, where a master&#39;s degree is normally the entry requirement.</p>
<p>When you intend to teach, where the degree requirements are specified and a diploma will not substitute.</p>
<p>When you may study abroad, where foreign institutions assess on credits and a diploma frequently falls short of what a master&#39;s-level entry requires.</p>
<h2>The question that settles it</h2>
<p>Get the requirement in writing from whoever imposes it, and read the exact word used. &quot;Postgraduate degree&quot; and &quot;postgraduate qualification&quot; are not the same phrase, and the difference decides which programme you should enrol in.</p>
<p>If nobody is imposing a requirement and this is for your own development, the diploma&#39;s shorter duration is a genuine advantage and there is no reason to spend the extra year.</p>
<h2>One more option</h2>
<p>Some universities allow a PG diploma to be carried forward into the related master&#39;s degree, with the diploma&#39;s credits counting toward it. Where that exists it is the best of both: a useful qualification in a year, with the option to complete the degree later without starting again. Ask whether the pathway exists before enrolling, because it has to be planned at the start.</p>
<p>Tell us what the qualification is for and we will tell you which of the two satisfies it.</p>
`;export{e as html};